Yes... I don't intend to shut up about proper quoting practice either,
mind. :-)

>I agree that number of characters is really a non-issue, but I brought it
>up because the only thing I saw in rfc1855 on signature length was the
>following:
[bandwidth]

Yes; the bandwidth supposition is widely propagated even now, but frankly
it's bunk. One doesn't see, even from the Elder Days when bandwidth was in
genuinely short supply, suggestions that people frex minimise the length
of message-IDs or refrain from inserting spurious extra headers such as
X-Newsreader, with anything like the frequency with which signatures are
claimed to waste bandwidth, even though we are talking about pinching
similar numbers of bytes.

>Now, what deleterious effects do you suppose a "bogus page" due to my five
>line .sig below may have?  As far as I can see, it will at worst mean that
>the reader may have to press "Page Down" or equivalent one extra time if
>they want to see the bottom line of my signature.

The effect is marginally worse in a well-designed newsreader where a
single key means "page if possible, otherwise next article", because it
reduces the functionality to that of ill-designed newsreaders where there
is no such key.

Of course the impact per reader per article is very small, but there are
many readers and you post a lot of articles; and what people miss is that
the benefit of longer sig blocks is also very small. Some subset of
readers smile once at the quote; thereafter all it does is provide an
extra recognition point for who the poster is, and a McQuary signature is
as good for that as any other...
